                <pb facs="0310" n="278" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v06/pg278"/>

                <head>171. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>According to the memorandum of discussion at the 389th meeting of the National
                    Security Council on December 6, Allen
                        Dulles made the following remarks about the situation in Cuba:
                        <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Mr. Dulles believed that the
                            revolutionary situation in Cuba was rapidly becoming more critical. The
                                Batista Government was
                            consistently losing ground in its struggle with Castro’s rebels. Business firms were
                            now seriously impeded and many operations were closing down <pb
                                facs="0311" n="279" xml:id="pg279"/> altogether. In sum, it was
                            increasingly unlikely that Batista would be able to regain control of this
                            situation.” (Eisenhower Library, Whitman File, NSC Records)</p>
                    </quote></p>
            </div>
